## Why This Is Asked
Microsoft tests understanding of tokenization trade-offs and attention mechanisms in multilingual contexts - critical for products like Bing Chat and Azure AI services.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates discuss: 1) Byte-Pair Encoding with language-specific merges, 2) Vocabulary size vs token count trade-offs, 3) Adding language ID tokens, 4) Attention modifications like language-aware positional encoding or cross-lingual attention heads.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Custom tokenizer with language-aware merges
class MultilingualTokenizer {
  vocabulary: Map<string, number>
  languageMerges: Map<string, string[]>

  tokenize(text: string, lang: string): number[] {
    const tokens = []
    const merges = this.languageMerges.get(lang) || []
    // Apply language-specific BPE merges
    return this.applyBPE(text, merges)
  }
}

// Language-aware attention modification
class LanguageAwareAttention {
  forward(x: Tensor, langIds: Tensor): Tensor {
    const attention = this.multiHeadAttention(x)
    const langBias = this.getLanguageBias(langIds)
    return attention + langBias
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle rare characters or emojis in the vocabulary?
- What metrics would you use to evaluate tokenizer efficiency?
- How does this approach scale to 50+ languages?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests system design skills for production AI systems, understanding of trade-offs between accuracy and latency, and knowledge of evaluation metrics in real-world scenarios.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates will discuss: 1) Multi-stage evaluation architecture, 2) Confidence threshold tuning, 3) Fallback strategies, 4) Monitoring and alerting, 5) Cost optimization through early filtering.

## Code Example
```typescript
class HallucinationDetector {
  async evaluate(response: string, context: string) {
    const semanticScore = await this.semanticCheck(response, context);
    if (semanticScore > 0.9) return { confident: true, score: semanticScore };

    const factualScore = await this.factualVerification(response);
    const ensembleScore = this.ensembleWeighting(semanticScore, factualScore);

    return {
      confident: ensembleScore > 0.85,
      score: ensembleScore,
      needsReview: ensembleScore < 0.7
    };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle edge cases where the knowledge graph is incomplete?
- What metrics would you track to optimize the confidence thresholds?
- How would you design A/B testing for new detection algorithms?

